ble, or unresolved, never infer bark from resource name. A positive result requires an actual separable bark layer whose properties meet food-assay's bark-sheet requirements. Stripping requires that verified layer and an owned stone with hardness at least 8. Reserve at most 0.12 mass of that finite layer, not additional source stock; perform two work rounds costing 0.5 energy each. At each paid stage remove the corresponding reserved mass from the substrate, retain it as partially processed material, and update substrate mass and condition. Completion returns the actual recovered mass as bark only if it meets the measured requirements; bark quantity equals recovered mass/0.3, with no rounding-created material. Failed separation retains real fragments, not assay-grade sheets. Never grant bark in addition to unchanged branch mass; preserve substrate identity, source provenance, remaining wood, fragments and losses in a conserved ledger. Source gathering and extraction share locks and composition accounting so neither can duplicate material. No felling, source regeneration, or assay construction is included. Missing energy or temporarily inaccessible tool pauses without charges; global pauses preserve exact work. Action change, movement out of reach, substrate destruction or death cancels once: release surviving unremoved reservations to their original substrate and return removed material to its owner, or drop it at the work location if dead; never refund spent energy or restore removed layers. Store actual remaining work; cancelled partial material remains physical but cannot be completed for free. Declare bark-inspected, bark-strip-progress, bark-stripped and bark-strip-stopped, plus qualified information-confirmed events. Bind paid stages, evidence, material quantities and uncertainty to event records and replay. Migration adds nullable material-layer fields and conservation bookkeeping, not bark stock or positive suitability. Existing generic branch sources remain